## Solution Idea

**Structure of the operation.** A contiguous substring of a CBS that is itself a CBS is exactly a consecutive run of sibling subtrees in the forest representation. The "special reversal" (reverse + flip brackets) of a forest $t_1\dots t_k$ gives $\mathrm{mir}(t_k)\dots \mathrm{mir}(t_1)$, where mirroring a tree mirrors its children in reverse order.

So, on the tree/forest model the move is: **pick a node, pick a contiguous range of its children, reverse the range and mirror each subtree in it.**

**Consequences.**
- A range of length 1 is allowed → we can mirror any single subtree.
- A range of length 2 gives $(a,b)\to(\bar b,\bar a)$; combined with single mirrors we get plain adjacent swaps.

Hence at every node the children can be permuted **arbitrarily**, and each subtree can independently be replaced by anything reachable from it (which is closed under mirroring).

By induction, the reachable set of a tree is determined only by the *multiset of equivalence classes of its children*, i.e. by the tree viewed as an **unordered rooted tree**. Two trees are interchangeable iff they are isomorphic as unordered rooted trees.

Therefore, for a node with $m$ children whose isomorphism classes have multiplicities $k_1,k_2,\dots$, the number of orderings is $\dfrac{m!}{\prod k_i!}$, and the total answer is the product of this over all nodes (including a virtual root for the whole forest).

Check: `(())()` → virtual root has two non‑isomorphic children → $2!/(1!1!)=2$. ✔

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
typedef long long ll;
const ll MOD=998244353;
ll pw(ll b,ll e){ll r=1;b%=MOD;while(e){if(e&1)r=r*b%MOD;b=b*b%MOD;e>>=1;}return r;}
int main(){
    int n; 
    if(scanf("%d",&n)!=1)return 0;
    char*buf=new char[n+5];
    scanf("%s",buf);
    string S(buf);
    vector<vector<int>> ch(1);
    vector<int> st; st.push_back(0);
    for(char c: S){
        if(c=='('){ ch.push_back({}); int v=ch.size()-1; ch[st.back()].push_back(v); st.push_back(v);}
        else st.pop_back();
    }
    int m=ch.size();
    vector<ll> fact(n+2),inv(n+2);
    fact[0]=1; for(int i=1;i<=n+1;i++)fact[i]=fact[i-1]*i%MOD;
    inv[n+1]=pw(fact[n+1],MOD-2); for(int i=n+1;i>0;i--)inv[i-1]=inv[i]*i%MOD;
    vector<int> id(m,0);
    map<vector<int>,int> mp;
    ll ans=1;
    for(int v=m-1;v>=0;--v){
        vector<int> c;
        for(int u: ch[v]) c.push_back(id[u]);
        sort(c.begin(),c.end());
        int sz=c.size();
        ans = ans*fact[sz]%MOD;
        int i=0;
        while(i<sz){ int j=i; while(j<sz&&c[j]==c[i])j++; ans=ans*inv[j-i]%MOD; i=j; }
        auto it=mp.find(c);
        if(it!=mp.end()) id[v]=it->second;
        else { int nid=mp.size(); mp[c]=nid; id[v]=nid; }
    }
    printf("%lld\n",ans%MOD);
    return 0;
}
```

**Complexity:** parsing is $O(N)$; canonical labelling with a map of sorted child-id vectors is $O(N\log^2 N)$ overall, easily fast for $N\le 5000$.

